ow [W7,W8,W9] (3), pair W9 (2), and T1, F4 (2) \u2014 that's 7 tiles \u2014 too many.\n\nI think I keep confusing myself.\n\nLet me clarify:\n\n- Exposed melds: 3 melds \u2192 9 tiles committed\n- Hand: 5 tiles (after draw)\n- To win, the 5 hand tiles must contain within them the components for one additional meld and a pair, using exactly 5 tiles.\n\nSo the 5 tiles must be decomposable into:\n- Option A: a chow (3) + a pair (2)\n- Option B: a pung (3) + a pair (2)\n- Option C: two pairs and a singleton, but then need the singleton to complete a meld with exposed, but exposed are fixed.\n\nSince the three melds are already formed and exposed, the hand must contain one full meld and one full pair.\n\nYes.\n\nSo hand must have 3+2=5 tiles that form one meld and one pair.\n\nCurrently, after draw, hand has 5 tiles.\n\nCan it contain a meld and a pair?\n\nExamples:\n- If I have [W7,W8,W9] and [W9,W9] \u2192 but that would be W7,W8,W9,W9,W9 \u2192 five tiles: chow + pair\n  - Then I can declare win with [W7,W8,W9] as chow and W9\u00d72 as pair\n  - But W9 used in both? No \u2014 cannot reuse.\n\nThe chow uses one W9, the pair uses two W9s \u2014 so need three W9s and W7,W8 \u2014 five tiles: W7,W8,W9,W9,W9\n\nYes! So hand: W7, W8, W9\u00d73\n\nThen I can form:\n- Chow: [W7,W8,W9]\n- Pair: [W9,W9]\n\nValid.\n\nTiles used: W7, W8, W9\u00d73 \u2014 five tiles.\n\nPerfect.\n\nSimilarly, if I have W8\u00d72, W9\u00d72, and W7 \u2014 same thing.\n\nSo to win, I need to draw W7 or W8 or W9.\n\nSpecifically, to have at least three W9s and one W7 and one W8.\n\nCurrently have W8\u00d71, W9\u00d72\n\nSo if I draw W7 or W8 or W9, I can potentially win.\n\nCase 1: draw W9 \u2192 hand: W8, W9\u00d73, T1, F4\n- Can I form a meld and pair from these?\n- Possibility: pung [W9,W9,W9] and pair [W8,W8]? Only one W8\n- Or chow [W8,W9,W?] \u2014 need W7\n- No W7\n- So cannot form [W7,W8,W9]\n- Thus, no winning combination\n\nCase 2: draw W8 \u2192 hand: W8\u00d72, W9\u00d72, T1, F4\n- Can I 