\n\nUnshown: W7:3 \u2014 means 3 left (since one used), so available.\n\nBut I need W7 and another W9.\n\nBut I only have two W9s.\n\nUnless I can make pair of something else.\n\nAnother idea: discard T1 and F4 early, and aim for a hand like:\n- Melds: [B7,B8,B9], [W3,W4,W5], [T2,T3,T4], and [W7,W8,W9]\n- Pair: W9\n\nRequires: in hand after draw: W7, W8, W9, W9, and either T1 or F4 to discard\n\nBut I have to have the tiles.\n\nCurrently have W8, W9\u00d72, T1, F4\n\nIf I draw W7, I have W7, W8, W9\u00d72, T1, F4\n\nTo win, I need to discard one of T1 or F4, but I can't discard until after I've declared win.\n\nI can only win if the 5 tiles include the fourth meld and the pair.\n\nSo the fifth tile must be the odd one out.\n\nThere is no way with this composition to have 3 for a meld and 2 for a pair.\n\nFor example:\n- The only possible meld in hand is if I have three of a kind or a chow.\n\nPossible chow in hand: only [W7,W8,W9]\n\nTo have that, need W7, W8, W9\n\nAnd for pair, need two of something else.\n\nBut the something else are T1 and F4 \u2014 both singles.\n\nSo unless I have two T1 or two F4, impossible.\n\nTherefore, **I cannot win on this draw** no matter what I draw, because my hand will always contain at least two singletons (T1 and F4) that cannot form a pair together, and I lack a third W9 or other duplicates.\n\nSo I must discard one, and continue waiting.\n\nNow, the choice is which to discard to improve future chances.\n\nOptions:\n- Play W9: reduces W9 to one, harder to make pung or pair\n- Play W8: lose W8, cannot form [W7,W8,W9]\n- Play T1: orphaned tile, little value\n- Play F4: honor tile, but only one, and seat wind (North) might be useful if I get more\n\nBut F4 is North Wind, my seat wind.\n\nIf I keep it, and later draw F4, I can make a pair or pung, scoring \"Seat Wind\" (2 points).\n\nWhereas T1 is a terminal, but in a suit where I have [T2,T3,T4], so T1 could theoretically form [T1,T2,T3] but T2,T3 are used.\n\nSo T1 is useless.\n\nSimilarly, W8 and W9 are part of potential [W7,W8,W9].\n\nSo between W8, W9, T1, F4, the least valuable are T1 and F4.\n\nBut F4 has potential for Seat Wind pattern.\n\nT1 has no such potential.\n\nAlso, \"No Honors\" is 1 point, but I have F4, so if I discard it, I can later achieve \"No Honors\" if I win without honors.\n\nBut currently, keeping F4 gives option for \"Seat Wind\".\n\nMoreover, the across player has Peng'd J2, next player has no dragons, so dragons are not safe.\n\nBut F4 is wind.\n\nPrevailing wind is West (F3), so F3 scores \"Prevalent Wind\", but I don't have it.\n\nMy seat wind F4 scores 2 points if I have a pung of it.\n\nBut only one.\n\nSo better to discard T1.\n\nBut let's see the valid actions: I can Play T1.\n\nAlternatively, discard W9.\n\nBut W9\u00d72, so if I discard one, I still have one, can aim for pair.\n\nBut to win, I need to draw W7 and have [W7,W8,W9] and pair W9, but again, requires three W9s.\n\nUnless I have W8\u00d72.\n\nBut only one W8.\n\nAnother possibility: if I draw W8, then I have W8\u00d72, W9\u00d72, T1, F4\n\nThen I can form [W8,W8,W8] if I draw another W8, but only two now.\n\nNot enough.\n\nPerhaps the best is to discard T1, as it is the most useless tile.\n\nThen hand becomes: W8, W9\u00d72, F4\n\nThen if I draw W7, I can have W7, W8, W9\u00d72, F4\n\nStill cannot win, but closer.\n\nIf I draw W9, I have W8, W9\u00d73, F4 \u2014 then I can form pung [W9,W9,W9] and need pair \u2014 F4 and W8, no pair.\n\nUnless I have another F4 or W8.\n\nNot helpful.\n\nPerhaps the optimal is to discard F4, to try for a \"No Honors\" hand.\n\nBecause if I keep F4, I can never have \"No Honors\".\n\nWhereas \"Seat Wind\" requires a pung, which is unlikely.\n\n\"No Honors\" is 1 point, \"Seat Wind\" is 2 points, but pung of F4 is very hard.\n\nExpected value: higher to go for \"No Honors\" by discarding F4.\n\nAdditionally, I have [B7,B8,B9] which includes terminals, so \"All Simples\" is out.\n\nBut \"No Honors\" is possible.\n\n\