s pair.\n\nSo: **discard B2** \u2014 it's a singleton, not useful, and likely safe.\n\nBut wait: my hand is: [W8, W9, W9, T9, B2] \u2014 five tiles. I need to discard one to go back to four.\n\nAfter discarding B2, hand becomes: [W8, W9, W9, T9] \u2014 still no pair, but W9 pair is forming.\n\nThen I can draw and aim to pair W9 or T9.\n\nAlternatively, discard T9 \u2192 keep W8-W9-W9 \u2192 same.\n\nBut W8 is more central to middle numbers.\n\nWait \u2014 better to keep **W9 pair** and reduce singletons.\n\nSo: discard either **B2** or **T9** or **W8**.\n\nBut **B2** is in a suit where melds are already formed by others, and B1 is gone \u2014 reduces utility.\n\nAlso, **B2** could be slightly dangerous if someone is collecting lower Bamboos, but no evidence.\n\nHowever, **T9** and **W9** are both 9s \u2014 terminals \u2014 more likely to be used in winning hands.\n\nSo safest discard is **B2**.\n\nMoreover, **J2 is Peng\u2019d**, **F4 is heavily discarded**, **B1 gone**, so honors and terminals are being used \u2014 but B2 is safer than T9 or W9.\n\nWait \u2014 opposite: since B1 is all gone, **no one can complete a chow with B1-B2-B3** anymore? No \u2014 they can if they have B2-B3, waiting for B1 \u2014 but B1 is all gone \u2192 impossible. So **any chow involving B1 is dead**. So B2 cannot be used in 1-2-3. Only in 2-3-4.\n\nBut 2-3-4 requires B2, B3, B4.\n\nB3: only 1 unshown (original 4, one used in next player\u2019s chow, two discarded? Let\u2019s check:\n\nUnshown B3: 1\n\nDiscards: \n- My history: Play B3\n- Previous player: Play B3\n- Next player: Play B2, Play B1 \u2014 but not B3\n- Across: no B3\n- Me: played B3 once\n- Previous: played B3 once\n\nSo at least two B3 discarded. Unshown: 1 \u2192 so only one left.\n\nB4: unshown 2 \u2192 two left.\n\nSo B2-B3-B4 still possible if someone has B3-B4.\n\nBut no evidence.\n\nStill, B2 is less dangerous than T9 or W9, which are terminals and often in high-value hands.\n\nBut actually, **terminal tiles are more dangerous** because they appear in many scoring patterns (Outside Hand, All Terminals, etc.).\n\nTherefore, **B2 is safer to discard** than T9 or W9.\n\nW8 is also relatively safe.\n\nBut I want to keep W9 pair.\n\nSo between W8 and B2, which to discard?\n\nIf I discard W8, I keep W9-W9-T9 \u2014 two terminals.\n\nBut W8 could help someone with W6-W7 or W7-W9 wait? Unlikely.\n\nBut W8 is part of 6-7-8 or 7-8-9.\n\nNext player has W6-W7-W8 \u2014 they\u2019re done.\n\nAcross player discarded W8 \u2014 so probably doesn\u2019t need it.\n\nStill, W8 is safer than T9.\n\nBut I have only one W8.\n\nBest move: **discard B2**, because:\n- It\u2019s a singleton\n- In a suit where early numbers are depleted (B1 gone)\n- Less likely to complete a winning hand\n- Keeps my W9 pair intact for better wait\n\nAdditionally, I avoid discarding a terminal if not necessary.\n\nSo final decision: **Play B2**\n\nAnswer: Play B2"}
