er, so opponents may suspect involvement.\n- **Play W5**: 3 unshown, I hold 2 \u2192 1 remains. W5 is part of potential middle sequences. But note: W5-W6-W7 is hard to form (W7/W8 are both 0 left), so chows ending in W5 (like W3-W4-W5) are more plausible. Since I already have a [W3,W4,W5] chow, another set may not be useful to most players. Still, W5 is slightly riskier because it\u2019s a mid-number and commonly used.\n\nMoreover, the board shows aggressive kong usage (J2 konged and upgraded), and several melds are on the table. The player across has a full kong of J2 and pung of J1 \u2014 strong dragon hand. Next player has multiple bamboo chows, possibly going for flush or straight. Previous player has only a pung of B1 \u2014 possibly weak.\n\nReason: Among the options, **W4** is the most dangerous to keep because there are zero remaining W4s in the wall (I hold 1, 2 were played or melded), meaning no one can draw it. If an opponent needed W4 for a chow, they must already have it \u2014 so discarding it won\u2019t help them complete anything from the wall. Furthermore, W4 is sandwiched between W3 and W5, both of which are still partially live, but given the lack of W6 and W7, extensions beyond W5 are impossible. Thus, **W4 is functionally dead** and safest to discard. Retaining it offers no value as it cannot be drawn again.\n\nAnswer: Play W4"}
